    "group": "Provider",
    "keywords": "docs protocol README Protocol notes Incremental messages and portals Protocol notes PostgreSQL backend frames begin with a one-byte identifier followed by a big-endian 32-bit length. The length includes itself and excludes the identifier. Startup messages are exceptional: they have no identifier byte. `BlueTuskBackendMessageParser` deliberately accepts unknown identifiers and returns a zero-copy payload view. It rejects lengths below four and lengths above its configured maximum before waiting for or allocating the payload. Incremental messages and portals `BlueTuskProtocolConnection.ReadMessageHeader` and `ReadMessageHeaderAsync` separate frame validation from payload consumption. Callers can then drain the active payload in bounded chunks with `ReadMessagePayload` or `ReadMessagePayloadAsync`; another frame cannot be read until that payload is complete. This is the primitive used for large `DataRow` fields, so sequential consumers do not need to allocate a complete backend message. `BlueTuskSession.BeginPortal` and `BeginPreparedPortal` expose incremental portals. An unlimited portal uses PostgreSQL's unnamed portal and sends `Sync` in the initial write. A positive fetch size uses a generated named portal, sends `Execute` followed by `Flush`, resu"
